Min-Hyun Cho received the B.S. and M.S. degrees in aerospace engineering from the Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology, Daejeon, South Korea, in 2016 and 2018, respectively. Since 2018, he has been a Researcher with Agency for Defense Development, Daejeon, South Korea. His current research interests include flight dynamics, control and estimation application.
Min-Jea Tahk (Member, IEEE) received the B.S. degree in aerospace engineering from Seoul National University, Seoul, South Korea, 1976, and the M.S. and Ph.D. degrees in aerospace engineering from the University of Texas, Austin, TX, USA, in 1983 and 1986, respectively. He is currently a Professor Emeritus of Aerospace Engineering with the Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology (KAIST), Daejeon, South Korea. From 1976 to 1981, he was a Research Engineer with the Agency for Defense Development in Korea, and from 1986 to 1989, he was employed by Integrated Systems, Inc., Santa Clara, CA, USA, before he joined KAIST. His recent research interests include missile guidance laws, weapon-target assignment, multiple target tracking, and convex programming for real-time applications. Prof. Tahk was a Technical Editor for the IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON AEROSPACE AND ELECTRONIC SYSTEMS from 2011 to 2016. He had also served as the Editor-in-Chief for International Journal of Aeronautical and Space Sciences from 2015 to 2018.
